Because $A$ is sorted, the indices $j$ satisfying $A_j \ge 2 A_i$ will form a contiguous range (or suffix) of the array. Specifically, since $A$ is non-decreasing, if $A_j \ge 2 A_i$, then for all $k > j$, $A_k \ge A_j \ge 2 A_i$.
So for a fixed $i$, we need to find the smallest index $j$ such that $A_j \ge 2 A_i$. Let this index be $j_{min}$. Then all indices from $j_{min}$ to $N$ are valid. The number of such indices is $N - j_{min} + 1$. If no such $j$ exists, the count is 0.

We can find $j_{min}$ using binary search (specifically `bisect_left` in Python) for each $i$.
The complexity would be $O(N \log N)$. Given $N=5 \times 10^5$, $N \log N \approx 5 \times 10^5 \times 19 \approx 9.5 \times 10^6$, which is well within the time limit (usually 2 seconds, allowing $\approx 10^8$ operations).

Alternatively, since $A$ is sorted, as $i$ increases, $A_i$ increases, so $2 A_i$ increases. Thus, the threshold value $2 A_i$ increases. Consequently, the starting index $j_{min}$ for valid $j$'s will also be non-decreasing.
